Wood Handbook — Forest Products Laboratory, 2021 (@mat-wood-handbook: 0, is #reference, #part-of @mat-sources) { r_2SP2KMHCD78MQGZN69GKPGJ3WM
Forest Products Laboratory. *Wood Handbook—Wood as an Engineering Material*. General Technical Report FPL-GTR-282, 2021. Robert J. Ross, editor. [Official publication and download](https://research.fs.usda.gov/treesearch/62200), [chapter guide](https://research.fs.usda.gov/fpl/wood-handbook), [DOI](https://doi.org/10.2737/FPL-GTR-282).

The institutional citation gives 543 pages. Read chapters 3–5 on structure, moisture/physical properties and mechanical behavior; 6–7 on products and stress grades; and 10–13 on adhesives, composites and drying. The full twenty-chapter handbook extends into fastenings, structural analysis, deterioration, preservation, finishing, construction, fire, specialty treatment and sterilization. Those chapters support later processing and service investigations.

The official publication, chapter list and chapter 5 abstract were inspected on 30 October 2026. Chapter 5, by C. Adam Senalik and Benjamin Farber, is listed as 46 pages. After the initial download failed, the complete [GovInfo preserved PDF](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-A13-PURL-gpo158673/pdf/GOVPUB-A13-PURL-gpo158673.pdf) was retrieved outside the repository: 546 PDF pages versus the bibliographic 543-page citation. Selected chapter 10 passages on surfaces/bonding and chapter 13 passages on moisture, drying stress, defects and kiln history were inspected; the full handbook and every table were not reviewed. Chapter 10 is by Charles R. Frihart and Christopher G. Hunt; chapter 13 is by Richard Bergman. Retain edition, species, moisture, table and test conditions when using data.

This is authorized public access through the US Forest Service, not a claim that every credited illustration or incorporated component has unrestricted adaptation rights. Keep source media external. The handbook's reported specimen properties are not automatically the permitted values for a particular structure or jurisdiction.

The degradation section assigns chapters 14–16 in full. Chapter 14’s opening and contents were inspected in the preserved full PDF on 30 October 2026: Rachel Arango, Stan T. Lebow and Jessie A. Glaeser distinguish fungal, bacterial, insect and marine-borer damage. The full preservation and finishing chapters have not been checked here for current product or regulatory applicability.

